use std
into faceedit.amni
dialect js

This file is a library built for the web as a wasm module; its `main()` does nothing. JavaScript drives it through its exported functions (every name starting with fe_ is exported). It does the geometry decisions for direct face editing on a solid: deleting faces (the faces around them grow to close the gap), offsetting faces along their normals, and moving faces (a translation and a rotation about an axis). JavaScript splits the region around the faces into cells with OpenCascade; this module describes the faces' surfaces analytically (flat, cylinder, cone, sphere or torus), works out which side of each surface is solid, builds the moved or offset surfaces, sizes the extended surfaces that cut the region, tests which cell boundary sample points lie on which surface, and decides which cells change between solid and empty. Its only import line is `import math;`, ending with a semicolon. Every int used in real math is converted with `as real`, and a real used as an int with `as int`, since Helium has no real or int conversion calls.

```amni
extern fn fe_view_f64(id i32, xs real[:]) = "fe_view_f64";
extern fn fe_view_i32(id i32, xs i32[:]) = "fe_view_i32";
```

Module state. Ints, all starting at 0: `NF` (edited faces), `NB` (neighbor faces), `NK` (surface slots, 2 NF plus NB), `NS` (cell samples) and `NC` (cells). Growable f64 arrays: `IN` (64 inputs), `OUT` (64 outputs), `SD` (16 per slot: the surface descriptors), `OR` (6 per edited or neighbor face: a point on the face and its outward normal), `BD` (4 per slot: the parameter bounds umin, umax, vmin and vmax of the extended surface), `SP` (7 per sample: the cell index, the point and the cell's outward normal there) and `BV` (NC times NF: the signed distance of largest size from each new surface over each cell's samples). Growable i32 arrays: `CI` (NC flags, 1 when the cell was solid), `OS` (NS times NK on-surface flags, sample i and slot k at i times NK plus k), `FL` (NC flags, 1 when the cell changes), `HT` (NC times NF flags, the cell touches edited face j), `HP` (NC times NF flags, the cell touches new face j), `TF` (NC flags, the cell touches an edited face) and `CL` (NC flags, every sample of the cell lies on an edited face or a neighbor surface).

Slots. Slot k below NF is edited face k, slot NF plus j is the new (moved or offset) surface of edited face j, and slot 2 NF plus m is neighbor face m. A descriptor at 16 k holds: the type (1 flat, 2 cylinder, 3 cone, 4 sphere, 5 torus), the origin O (3 values at 16 k plus 1), the axis Z (3 values at 16 k plus 4), the X direction (3 at 16 k plus 7), the Y direction (3 at 16 k plus 10), the first radius r1 at 16 k plus 13 (cylinder, sphere and torus major radius, cone reference radius), the second value r2 at 16 k plus 14 (cone half-angle in radians, torus minor radius) and the side sign s at 16 k plus 15 (1.0 or -1.0). OR holds edited face j at 6 j and neighbor face m at 6 times (NF plus m).

Sizing helpers. `zf(xs, n, x)` takes an f64 growable array by reference: xs is emptied, then gets `reserve`(n), then is resized to n entries equal to x. `zi(xs, n, x)` does the same for an i32 growable array, resizing to n entries equal to x as i32. On the web an array that holds entries cannot grow past the room it first reserved, so every array is emptied before it is sized again. `at1(n)` returns n, or 1 when n is below 1.

The exported function `fe_views()`, declared with the export keyword with an i32 result, registers the arrays with `fe_view_f64`: 1 IN, 2 OUT, 3 SD, 4 OR, 5 BD, 6 SP; and with `fe_view_i32`: 20 CI, 21 OS, 22 FL. It returns 0.

The exported function `fe_init()`, declared with the export keyword with an i32 result, sizes IN to 64 zeros and OUT to 64 zeros with `zf`, calls `fe_views()` and returns 0.

The exported function `fe_size(nf, nb)`, declared with the export keyword with two i32 parameters (each converted with `as int` at the top) and an i32 result, sets NF to nf, NB to nb and NK to 2 nf plus nb, then sizes with `zf` to zeros, each size raised to at least 1 with `at1`: SD to 16 NK, OR to 6 times (nf plus nb) and BD to 4 NK. It calls `fe_views()` and returns 0.

The exported function `fe_cells(ns, nc)`, declared with the export keyword with two i32 parameters (each converted with `as int` at the top) and an i32 result, sets NS to ns and NC to nc, then sizes, each size raised to at least 1 with `at1`: with `zf` SP to 7 ns and BV to nc times NF, both zeros; with `zi` CI to nc, OS to ns times NK, FL to nc, HT and HP to nc times NF, TF and CL to nc, all zeros. It calls `fe_views()` and returns 0.

Surface helpers, each taking a slot k and a point (px, py, pz). With v the point less O, zc the dot product of v with Z, w the part of v across the axis (v less zc times Z) and rr its length:

`raw(k, px, py, pz)` returns the unsigned-side distance: type 1 returns zc; type 2 returns rr minus r1; type 3 returns (rr minus (r1 plus zc times the tangent of r2)) times the cosine of r2; type 4 returns the length of v minus r1; type 5 returns the square root of ((rr minus r1) squared plus zc squared) minus r2.

`sd(k, px, py, pz)` returns s times raw(k, px, py, pz): positive outside the solid, negative inside.

`gdot(k, px, py, pz, nx, ny, nz)` returns the dot product of the direction in which raw grows at the point with the vector (nx, ny, nz): type 1 uses Z; type 2 uses w over rr (0.0 when rr is not above 1e-12); type 3 uses w over rr less the tangent of r2 times Z (w over rr counted as zero when rr is not above 1e-12); type 4 uses v; type 5 uses v less r1 times w over rr (w over rr counted as zero when rr is not above 1e-12).

`rotv(ax, ay, az, wx, wy, wz, t, c)` rotates the vector (ax, ay, az) by angle t (radians) about the unit axis (wx, wy, wz) with Rodrigues' formula: a times the cosine of t, plus the cross product w cross a times the sine of t, plus w times (w dot a) times (1.0 minus the cosine of t); it returns component c (0, 1 or 2) of the result.

`bounds(k, g)` fills BD[4 k] to BD[4 k plus 3] for slot k over the box from (IN[13], IN[14], IN[15]) to (IN[16], IN[17], IN[18]) grown by g on every side. pad is 0.05 times the length of the grown box's diagonal plus 1e-7. Over the 8 box corners, with v the corner less O: U ranges over the dot product of v with X, V over v with Y and Z over v with Z. Type 1 stores (smallest U minus pad, largest U plus pad, smallest V minus pad, largest V plus pad). Type 2 stores (0.0, 2 pi, smallest Z minus pad, largest Z plus pad). Type 3 stores (0.0, 2 pi, a, b) where a is (smallest Z minus pad) over the cosine of r2 and b is (largest Z plus pad) over the cosine of r2; then when the absolute value of the sine of r2 is above 1e-9, with va equal to minus r1 over the sine of r2, a is raised to va when va is below 0.0, and otherwise b is lowered to va. Type 4 stores (0.0, 2 pi, minus pi over 2, pi over 2). Type 5 stores (0.0, 2 pi, 0.0, 2 pi).

The exported function `fe_prep()`, declared with the export keyword with an i32 result, gets the descriptors ready once JavaScript has filled SD for the edited slots (0 to NF minus 1) and the neighbor slots (2 NF onward), OR, and IN. Inputs: IN[0] the mode (0 delete, 1 offset, 2 move), IN[1] the offset distance d, IN[2], IN[3] and IN[4] the move translation, IN[5], IN[6] and IN[7] a point on the rotation axis, IN[8], IN[9] and IN[10] the rotation axis direction, and IN[11] the rotation angle in degrees.

1. Sides: for each edited face j below NF (slot j, sample at OR[6 j]) and each neighbor face m below NB (slot 2 NF plus m, sample at OR[6 times (NF plus m)]), the slot's s becomes 1.0 when gdot at the sample point with the sample normal is at least 0.0, and -1.0 otherwise.
2. New surfaces: each slot NF plus j becomes a copy of all 16 values of slot j. In mode 1, with d IN[1] and s the side sign: type 1 moves O by d times s along Z; types 2 and 4 add d times s to r1; type 3 adds d times s over the cosine of r2 to r1; type 5 adds d times s to r2. When a type 2 or 4 r1, or a type 5 r2, is not above 1e-9 after that, it returns minus (j plus 1). In mode 2, with the axis direction normalized (no rotation when its length is not above 1e-12 or the angle is 0.0) and t the angle in radians: O becomes the rotation of (O less the axis point) plus the axis point, plus the translation; Z, X and Y are each rotated with rotv.
3. Bounds: with g equal to the absolute value of IN[1] when the mode is 1 and 0.0 otherwise, bounds(j, g) for each edited slot j, bounds(NF plus j, 0.0) for each new slot, and bounds(2 NF plus m, 0.0) for each neighbor slot.

Returns 0.

The exported function `fe_onsurf()`, declared with the export keyword with an i32 result, tests each sample i below NS (point at SP[7 i plus 1] to SP[7 i plus 3]) against each slot k below NK: OS[i times NK plus k] is 1 when the absolute value of sd(k, point) is below the tolerance IN[12], and 0 otherwise. New slots (NF to 2 NF minus 1) are only tested in modes 1 and 2 and stay 0 in mode 0. It returns the count of flags set to 1.

The exported function `fe_decide()`, declared with the export keyword with an i32 result, runs after JavaScript has cleared the flags of samples that lie on an edited or new surface but outside the face itself. It fills TF and HT with 0, CL with 1, HP with 0 and BV with 0.0. For each sample i with cell c equal to SP[7 i] as int and point p: on is 0; for each edited slot j below NF with OS[i times NK plus j] equal to 1, TF[c] becomes 1, HT[c times NF plus j] becomes 1 and on becomes 1; for each new slot j with OS[i times NK plus NF plus j] equal to 1, HP[c times NF plus j] becomes 1; for each neighbor slot m with OS[i times NK plus 2 NF plus m] equal to 1, on becomes 1; when on is 0, CL[c] becomes 0. In modes 1 and 2, for each j below NF, with v equal to sd(NF plus j, p), BV[c times NF plus j] becomes v when the absolute value of v is above the absolute value of BV[c times NF plus j].

Then for each cell c below NC, FL[c] starts at 0. In mode 0, FL[c] is 1 when TF[c] is 1 and CL[c] is 1. In modes 1 and 2: the cell uses the HT row when TF[c] is 1 and the HP row otherwise; when no flag in that row is set the cell keeps its state; otherwise the cell becomes solid (nw is 1) when BV[c times NF plus j] is below 0.0 for every j whose flag is set in that row, and empty (nw is 0) otherwise, and FL[c] is 1 when nw differs from CI[c]. OUT[0] counts the cells that become solid (FL 1 and CI 0) and OUT[1] the cells that become empty (FL 1 and CI 1). It returns OUT[0] plus OUT[1] as int.

The `main()` function does nothing.
